 "note": "Same 60 dev ids (20 per tier, seed 140) for every run, offline, OCR-first, one run at a time on the same Windows laptop. 'off' = phi.handwriting.enabled false (identical to the pipeline without the feature). The reader runs on CPU (torch CPU build), so the GPU peak equals the idle baseline. Latency is wall-clock per page of the whole case. All 14 added detections come from credential-anchor crops; reads are traced as counts, timings and hashes only. Technologist GT boxes include ', RT' and span both lines when the sign-off wraps, so a detection must cover the whole sign-off to count (COVER 0.5). Where the gain comes from: with the credential context any non-empty read of an anchor crop yields a name span, so the recall gain mostly reflects the crop rule (the words between a boundary and an RT/MD credential that the rules missed because OCR garbled the dash or the name shape); the reader confirms the crop holds writing and supplies the text the detector checks. The off run reproduces the earlier Tesseract dev numbers on these ids exactly (PHI recall 0.8889, precision 0.7751; runs/ocr_tess)."
